@base <https://ds-labs-org.github.io/ds-odrl-compliance-rdf/cases/recipient-refined-prohibition-raw-derived-split-01> .
@prefix :      <#> .
@prefix dsc:   <https://ds-labs-org.github.io/ds-odrl-compliance-rdf/ns#> .
@prefix odrl:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/odrl/2/> .
@prefix report: <https://w3id.org/force/compliance-report#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# Derived, and anonymized, from a real-world EDC-connector ODRL policy (a
# genuine third-party API terms-of-use document) reviewed in this project's
# chat session. Every real company/domain/project/contact name has been
# replaced or omitted; only the STRUCTURAL pattern is carried over: raw data
# may be retrieved but never displayed, only a conceptually separate
# DERIVED asset (an aggregate/report) may be shown or distributed, a
# distribution prohibition on the raw asset is gated by a `recipient neq
# <authorized consumer>` action refinement, each prohibition carries its
# own `odrl:remedy` (delete), and one obligation is time-bound
# (`elapsedTime lteq PT24H`) while another is an always-outstanding,
# unconstrained duty. Not carried over from the source policy: the
# aggregate/derive/print action group is represented by one action
# (`odrl:derive`) rather than all three, the display/present/distribute/
# archive group on the derived asset by one action (`odrl:distribute`)
# rather than all four, the second (archive/index) and third
# (sell/transfer/give/grantUse) raw-asset prohibitions, and the
# reviewPolicy/acceptTracking obligations -- all of them would exercise the
# same shapes already covered elsewhere in this corpus (an unconstrained,
# always-outstanding duty; a plain unrefined prohibition with a remedy)
# without demonstrating anything new, so this fixture keeps to the subset
# that is actually distinctive: the raw-vs-derived action split, the
# refinement-gated recipient check, and the time-bound/unconstrained duty
# contrast.
#
# Engine-verified against ds-odrl-engine-rs at commit 8609ed3 (one commit
# past tag v0.23.3) via a scratch `engine::evaluate_request_detailed` call
# over the hand-transcribed wire JSON equivalent of this file's own
# :request (ds-odrl-engine-rs-work/engine/examples/scratch_dsc_verify.rs,
# untracked, not committed). Confirmed live, exactly as published below:
# the two permissions whose own action doesn't match this request
# (read, derive) and the one whose target doesn't (distribute on the
# DERIVED asset, while this request targets the raw one) are all
# NotAttempted/Inactive; the plain display prohibition doesn't match this
# request's action either (NotAttempted/Inactive), so its own remedy never
# fires (Inactive/Unknown/NonSet); the refined distribute prohibition DOES
# fire (Active/Attempted/Performed/Violated, its own
# `report:ConstraintReport`-equivalent refinement premise Satisfied because
# claimed recipient "party-outside" != :party-consumer), and its remedy
# fires with it (Active/Unperformed/Violated); the unconstrained attribute
# obligation is always outstanding (Active/Unperformed/Violated) while the
# time-bound delete obligation is satisfied by the supplied elapsedTime
# claim ("PT10H" <= "PT24H": Active/Performed/Fulfilled); and the overall
# `Response.decision` is "Deny" (the firing prohibition, with no colliding
# permission anywhere in the policy for this same action+target, so
# `odrl:conflict` is never even reached).

:testcase a dsc:TestCase ;
    dct:title "A real-world-derived policy: raw data may be read/derived but never displayed; only the derived asset may be distributed; distributing the RAW asset is prohibited except to the one authorized recipient (an action refinement), each prohibition carries its own remedy, and an unconstrained attribution duty contrasts with a time-bound deletion duty" ;
    dct:created "2026-09-24T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dct:creator <mailto:shared-claude@unxwares.com> ;
    dsc:request :request ;
    dsc:expectedOutcome :report-a ;
    dsc:expectedDecision "Deny" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Assets, parties
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:asset-raw a odrl:Asset .
# The raw API data: may be read and transformed, but never shown or
# supplied onward except back to its own authorized consumer.

:asset-derived-report a odrl:Asset .
# A conceptually SEPARATE asset produced by transforming :asset-raw (e.g.
# an aggregate or a report) -- may be displayed/distributed freely, since
# what is shown is the outcome of the transformation, never the raw values
# it was built from.

:party-consumer a odrl:Party .
# The one party authorized to receive the raw asset -- also this policy's
# own odrl:assignee, and the rightOperand this file's refinement checks
# the request's own "recipient" claim against.

:party-outside a odrl:Party .
# A DIFFERENT party -- used only as this request's own "recipient" claim
# value, to demonstrate the refinement actually firing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile -- every action any rule below names must be declared here
# (ds-odrl-engine-rs's own `first_unrecognized_action` check), or the whole
# request errors rather than denies.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:profile a odrl:Profile ;
    odrl:action odrl:read, odrl:derive, odrl:distribute, odrl:display,
                odrl:delete, odrl:attribute .
    # No dsc:dutyMode/dsc:behaviour -- the engine's own no-profile defaults
    # (Advise/Open) are not load-bearing here: no permission carries a
    # gating odrl:duty, and every rule under test is explicit about its own
    # action/target, so neither axis changes this file's own outcome.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Permissions -- the raw-vs-derived split. Two permissions on the raw
# asset (read it, transform it into the derived report); one permission on
# the DERIVED asset alone for the very action (distribute) this request
# attempts on the RAW asset -- inapplicable here only because the target
# differs, which is exactly the split this fixture demonstrates.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:perm-read-raw a odrl:Permission ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw ;
    odrl:action odrl:read .
    # Retrieving the raw values via the API -- permitted outright.

:perm-derive a odrl:Permission ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw ;
    odrl:action odrl:derive .
    # Stands in for the source policy's aggregate/derive/print group:
    # transforming the raw asset into :asset-derived-report.

:perm-distribute-derived a odrl:Permission ;
    odrl:target :asset-derived-report ;
    odrl:action odrl:distribute .
    # Stands in for display/present/distribute/archive on the DERIVED
    # asset only -- deliberately unconstrained, to contrast with the
    # refined prohibition below that covers the SAME action name
    # (odrl:distribute) but is scoped to the RAW asset instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prohibitions -- both on the raw asset, both remedied by deletion.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:proh-display-raw a odrl:Prohibition ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw ;
    odrl:action odrl:display ;
    odrl:remedy :remedy-delete-display .
    # Showing the raw values on screen, in any static or transient
    # rendition, is never permitted -- only the derived report may be
    # shown (:perm-distribute-derived's sibling permissions, not exercised
    # by this file). Not the action this request attempts, so this rule
    # itself stays NotAttempted/Inactive here -- present to show the
    # structural contrast with :perm-distribute-derived above, not to fire.

:remedy-delete-display a odrl:Duty ;
    odrl:action odrl:delete ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw .

:proh-distribute-raw-refined a odrl:Prohibition ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw ;
    odrl:action [
        rdf:value odrl:distribute ;
        odrl:refinement [
            a odrl:Constraint ;
            odrl:leftOperand odrl:recipient ;
            odrl:operator odrl:neq ;
            odrl:rightOperand :party-consumer
        ]
    ] ;
    odrl:remedy :remedy-delete-distribute .
    # Supplying the raw asset to any recipient OTHER than the one
    # authorized consumer is prohibited -- the refinement is what makes
    # this conditional rather than a blanket ban: distributing it BACK to
    # :party-consumer itself would not trigger this rule at all. THIS is
    # the rule this request actually attempts and violates.

:remedy-delete-distribute a odrl:Duty ;
    odrl:action odrl:delete ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Obligations -- policy-level, always "in force" regardless of the
# requested action (docs/vocabulary-spec.md section 5 / the worked
# example's own duty-notify convention) -- the time-bound/unconstrained
# contrast this fixture is partly about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:duty-attribute a odrl:Duty ;
    odrl:action odrl:attribute ;
    odrl:attributedParty :party-consumer .
    # No odrl:constraint -- like every other unconstrained duty in this
    # corpus, this is NOT vacuously satisfied: duty_satisfied requires at
    # least one constraint, so this duty is always outstanding, by design.

:duty-delete-window a odrl:Duty ;
    odrl:action odrl:delete ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw ;
    odrl:constraint [
        a odrl:Constraint ;
        odrl:leftOperand odrl:elapsedTime ;
        odrl:operator odrl:lteq ;
        odrl:rightOperand "PT24H"^^xsd:duration
    ] .
    # Retrieved raw data must be deleted within 24 hours of retrieval -- a
    # cache-retention limit, not a requirement to destroy the (separate)
    # derived report. Genuinely satisfiable, and satisfied here, unlike
    # :duty-attribute above: the contrast this fixture demonstrates.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:policy-a a odrl:Offer ;
    odrl:assigner <did:web:provider.example> ;
    odrl:assignee :party-consumer ;
    odrl:conflict odrl:prohibit ;
    odrl:permission :perm-read-raw, :perm-derive, :perm-distribute-derived ;
    odrl:prohibition :proh-display-raw, :proh-distribute-raw-refined ;
    odrl:obligation :duty-attribute, :duty-delete-window .
    # odrl:conflict odrl:prohibit mirrors the source policy's own
    # "conflict": "prohibit" but is NOT load-bearing in this fixture: no
    # permission anywhere in this policy grants odrl:distribute on
    # :asset-raw, so the two rules never genuinely collide and
    # odrl:conflict is never consulted (confirmed live -- see the
    # engine-verification note above).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Request -- attempts odrl:distribute on the RAW asset, to a recipient
# ("party-outside") who is NOT the authorized consumer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:claim-recipient a dsc:ClaimAssertion ;
    dsc:key "recipient" ;
    rdf:value :party-outside .
    # Bare environment fact, resolved via odrl:recipient's own native
    # local-name resolution rule (section 4.5) -- its value happens to be
    # a resource (the actual recipient of THIS request), stringified by
    # IRI per the comparison convention, not the request's identity claim.

:claim-elapsed a dsc:ClaimAssertion ;
    dsc:key "elapsedTime" ;
    rdf:value "PT10H"^^xsd:duration .
    # Bare environment fact, resolved via odrl:elapsedTime's own native
    # local-name resolution rule -- 10 hours, within the 24-hour window
    # :duty-delete-window's own constraint checks.

:request a dsc:Request ;
    odrl:target :asset-raw ;
    odrl:action odrl:distribute ;
    dsc:profile :profile ;
    dsc:policy :policy-a ;
    dsc:claim :claim-recipient, :claim-elapsed .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expected outcome
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:the-ask a odrl:Request ;
    odrl:prohibition [ a odrl:Prohibition ; odrl:action odrl:distribute ; odrl:target :asset-raw ] .

:report-a a report:PolicyReport ;
    report:policy :policy-a ;
    report:policyRequest :the-ask ;
    report:ruleReport :rr-a-perm-read , :rr-a-perm-derive , :rr-a-perm-distribute-derived ,
                       :rr-a-proh-display , :rr-a-remedy-display ,
                       :rr-a-proh-distribute-refined , :rr-a-remedy-distribute ,
                       :rr-a-duty-attribute , :rr-a-duty-delete-window .

# --- raw-asset permissions: neither's own action is the one requested ---

:rr-a-perm-read a report:PermissionReport ;
    report:rule :perm-read-raw ;
    report:activationState report:Inactive ;
    report:attemptState report:NotAttempted ;
    report:performanceState report:Unperformed ;
    report:deonticState report:NonSet ;
    report:premiseReport :pr-a-read-target, :pr-a-read-action .

:pr-a-read-target a report:TargetReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Satisfied .
:pr-a-read-action a report:ActionReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Unsatisfied .

:rr-a-perm-derive a report:PermissionReport ;
    report:rule :perm-derive ;
    report:activationState report:Inactive ;
    report:attemptState report:NotAttempted ;
    report:performanceState report:Unperformed ;
    report:deonticState report:NonSet ;
    report:premiseReport :pr-a-derive-target, :pr-a-derive-action .

:pr-a-derive-target a report:TargetReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Satisfied .
:pr-a-derive-action a report:ActionReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Unsatisfied .

# --- derived-asset permission: SAME action (distribute) as the request,
# but a DIFFERENT target -- the raw-vs-derived split, made concrete ---

:rr-a-perm-distribute-derived a report:PermissionReport ;
    report:rule :perm-distribute-derived ;
    report:activationState report:Inactive ;
    report:attemptState report:NotAttempted ;
    report:performanceState report:Unperformed ;
    report:deonticState report:NonSet ;
    report:premiseReport :pr-a-dist-derived-target, :pr-a-dist-derived-action .

:pr-a-dist-derived-target a report:TargetReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Unsatisfied .
:pr-a-dist-derived-action a report:ActionReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Satisfied .

# --- plain display prohibition: not the requested action, so it never
# fires here, and its own remedy therefore never fires either ---

:rr-a-proh-display a report:ProhibitionReport ;
    report:rule :proh-display-raw ;
    report:activationState report:Inactive ;
    report:attemptState report:NotAttempted ;
    report:performanceState report:Unknown ;
    report:deonticState report:NonSet ;
    report:premiseReport :pr-a-display-target, :pr-a-display-action .

:pr-a-display-target a report:TargetReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Satisfied .
:pr-a-display-action a report:ActionReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Unsatisfied .

:rr-a-remedy-display a report:DutyReport ;
    report:rule :remedy-delete-display ;
    report:activationState report:Inactive ;
    report:performanceState report:Unknown ;
    report:deonticState report:NonSet .
    # No report:attemptState -- outside report:DutyReport's declared domain.

# --- the refined distribute prohibition: genuinely fires. The action
# premise reports Satisfied because the refinement itself is Satisfied
# (claimed recipient "party-outside" != :party-consumer) -- this corpus's
# established convention folds a satisfied action refinement into the
# SAME report:ActionReport as ordinary action coverage (see
# inherited-agreement-duty-chain-01.ttl's :perm-print-clean), not a
# separate premise node. No genuinely colliding permission exists
# anywhere in this policy for odrl:distribute+:asset-raw, so
# odrl:conflict is never reached and this prohibition is simply Active. ---

:rr-a-proh-distribute-refined a report:ProhibitionReport ;
    report:rule :proh-distribute-raw-refined ;
    report:activationState report:Active ;
    report:attemptState report:Attempted ;
    report:performanceState report:Performed ;
    report:deonticState report:Violated ;
    report:premiseReport :pr-a-dist-refined-target, :pr-a-dist-refined-action .

:pr-a-dist-refined-target a report:TargetReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Satisfied .
:pr-a-dist-refined-action a report:ActionReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Satisfied .

:rr-a-remedy-distribute a report:DutyReport ;
    report:rule :remedy-delete-distribute ;
    report:activationState report:Active ;
    report:performanceState report:Unperformed ;
    report:deonticState report:Violated .
    # The one remedy that actually fires in this fixture: the prohibition
    # it belongs to is genuinely Active/Violated, and this remedy duty
    # carries no odrl:constraint of its own, so it is unperformed/violated
    # for the same reason :duty-attribute below always is.

# --- policy-level obligations: always in force, independent of the
# requested action -- the time-bound/unconstrained contrast ---

:rr-a-duty-attribute a report:DutyReport ;
    report:rule :duty-attribute ;
    report:activationState report:Active ;
    report:performanceState report:Unperformed ;
    report:deonticState report:Violated .
    # Unconstrained: never satisfiable, always outstanding, by design.

:rr-a-duty-delete-window a report:DutyReport ;
    report:rule :duty-delete-window ;
    report:activationState report:Active ;
    report:performanceState report:Performed ;
    report:deonticState report:Fulfilled ;
    report:premiseReport :pr-a-delete-window-constraint .

:pr-a-delete-window-constraint a report:ConstraintReport ; report:satisfactionState report:Satisfied .
    # elapsedTime "PT10H" <= rightOperand "PT24H" -- genuinely satisfiable,
    # and satisfied by this request's own claim, unlike :duty-attribute.
